Classify a reset or stop of a terminal or unknown stream by the ID's state.

Applies to picoquic a602e60babea48da7849169d716aaf68684e66d6, after 0002,
0006, 0007, 0008 and 0009; it also applies without 0007.

picoquic_reset_stream_at and picoquic_stop_sending answered every stream they
no longer hold with PICOQUIC_ERROR_INVALID_STREAM_ID, whether the ID was
opened and has since been retired or was never opened, and the stop cleared
the stream's application context before refusing a part the peer had reset.
Stream IDs are never reused (RFC 9000 Section 2.1), and picoquic already keeps
each type's creation boundary.

Both now check the 62-bit range and the direction first: a reset needs a
sending part (not a peer-initiated unidirectional stream, Section 19.4), a stop
a receiving part (not a local unidirectional one, Section 19.5); otherwise
PICOQUIC_ERROR_INVALID_STREAM_ID, for a live stream as well. An absent ID below
its type's boundary answers PICOQUIC_ERROR_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED: that part is
terminal and no frame is sent (Section 3.3); any other absent ID stays
PICOQUIC_ERROR_INVALID_STREAM_ID. A stop of a live stream whose receiving part
is terminal -- reset by the peer, or read up to its final size (Section 3.2) --
answers PICOQUIC_ERROR_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED before the stream or its context
changes. The reset of a live stream keeps 0002's rules. Nothing is created,
allocated or queued on any refusal. PICOQUIC_TERMINAL_STREAM_ALREADY_CLOSED
advertises the contract in picoquic.h.
Tests: adapters/picoquic/tests/test_pq_stop_release.c (reset and stop
contract rows) and test_pq_hold_input.c (call order, bounded).

Upstream picoquic/streams.c and picoquic/frames.c are unchanged since the pin
as of 2026-10-16 (master c124cbe975f71b150ea929d325804335d2bdaa34, 15 commits
ahead; the audit commands are recorded in 0009).

